<%#
  The Settings window's built-in changelog (issue #1011).

  Replaces the <iframe> of https://iracedeck.com/changelog/ that used to sit on
  the What's New tab. That iframe showed whatever the website said — which can be
  a version the user does not have — needed internet, and dropped a white website
  page into a dark window.

  The notes rendered here are the ones this build ships: `changelog.json` is
  generated from `packages/website/src/content/docs/changelog.mdx` and the
  in-development fragments in `changelog.d/` by `pnpm generate:changelog-data`,
  which turbo runs before every plugin build (the file is gitignored, #1386), so
  the single source of truth is unchanged and the pane works offline. Bullet
  `items` are HTML that the generator already
  escaped (see scripts/lib/changelog-inline-html.mjs) — which is why they are
  emitted raw here rather than re-escaped.

  Releases come out newest first, so the release the user is running is normally
  the first thing on the pane: no scrolling, and nothing to run at load. The
  INSTALLED badge names it explicitly, and a build whose version has no section
  yet (a `-dev` build on the next version) says so, rather than marking nothing
  and leaving the reader to wonder which of these they have.

  The release still in development is the undated one at the top. Normally it
  carries the next version (`## 2.5.0` / `_Unreleased_`, shown with an
  "Unreleased" date chip) and is the one a `-dev` build is badged on. Between a
  stable fold and the next version bump it is titled `Unreleased` itself, with no
  date line (#1398): that card gets no chip, so the word is not printed twice,
  and no badge, since the build still reports the folded version, whose dated
  card sits right below it and is badged there.

  Since #1016 the pane also carries ONE runtime: `<ird-update-notice>` asks the
  plugin whether a newer version has been published and, if so, renders a
  banner and the newer releases into the list below — above the installed one,
  badged "Not installed". Everything below stays exactly as it is when that
  check is off, fails, or finds nothing, which is what keeps the offline
  property #1011 bought.

  Included by settings-window.ejs only; its styling lives with the rest of the
  window's CSS on that page.
%>
<%
  var __changelog = require('./data/changelog.json');
  var __releases = __changelog.releases || [];
  // Match on the numeric version: a build reports `2.5.0-dev.0` while the
  // changelog section is `## 2.5.0`. The fragment loader strips the suffix the
  // same way to name the in-development section (scripts/lib/changelog-fragments.mjs).
  var __version = typeof version === 'undefined' ? '' : String(version);
  var __installed = __version.replace(/[-+].*$/, '');
  var __hasInstalled = __releases.some(function (r) { return r.version === __installed; });
-%>
<%# The upstream check (#1016). Mounted unconditionally — even a build with no
    notes of its own can be behind a published release — and renders nothing
    unless the plugin reports one. %>
<ird-update-notice list="sw-changelog"></ird-update-notice>
<% if (__releases.length === 0) { -%>
<p class="sw-cl-empty">Release notes are not available in this build.</p>
<div class="sw-changelog" id="sw-changelog"></div>
<% } else { -%>
<% if (!__hasInstalled && __version) { -%>
<p class="sw-cl-note">No release notes for version <%= __version %> yet — the releases below are the ones already published.</p>
<% } -%>
<div class="sw-changelog" id="sw-changelog">
<% __releases.forEach(function (release, __index) { -%>
<% var __isInstalled = release.version === __installed; -%>
<%
  // An undated release is "Unreleased" only at the TOP of a strictly newest-first
  // list — that is the one section still in development. Further down, an absent
  // date means the entry predates the date convention (`## 0.13.0`), and labelling
  // a release that shipped months ago "Unreleased" is simply wrong; it shows no
  // date at all instead.
  //
  // A release titled `Unreleased` (the window after a stable fold, #1398) gets no
  // chip either: its title already says it. EJS cannot import, so this literal
  // is kept by hand equal to UNRELEASED_LABEL in scripts/lib/changelog-parse.mjs;
  // settings-window-changelog-partial.test.ts builds its fixture from that
  // constant, so the two drifting apart fails the test.
  var __isUnreleasedLabel = release.version === 'Unreleased';
  var __date = release.date || (__index === 0 && !__isUnreleasedLabel ? 'Unreleased' : '');
-%>
	<article class="sw-cl-release<%= __isInstalled ? ' installed' : '' %>">
		<div class="sw-cl-head">
			<h3 class="sw-cl-version"><%= release.version %></h3>
<% if (__date) { -%>
			<span class="sw-cl-date"><%= __date %></span>
<% } -%>
<% if (__isInstalled) { -%>
			<span class="sw-cl-badge">Installed</span>
<% } -%>
		</div>
<% release.categories.forEach(function (category) { -%>
		<h4 class="sw-cl-category"><%= category.title %></h4>
		<ul class="sw-cl-items">
<% category.items.forEach(function (item) { -%>
			<li><%- item %></li>
<% }); -%>
		</ul>
<% }); -%>
	</article>
<% }); -%>
</div>
<% } -%>
